Bold7 Camera
============

This program is a modified version of BenOS Camera, written by Rudi Timmermans
(https://github.com/ruditimmermans/BenOSCamera), taken at the tag v3.6, commit
3f0d633d672506ba8e81979b6b448179780ba97c. The whole history and authorship of the original
are kept in this repository.

Like the original, it is free software under the GNU General Public License, version 3
(LICENSE). It comes with no warranty.

I, Arthur Michon (codexofc on GitHub), changed it from 17 November 2026 for Bold7, a BlackBerry
OS 7.1 interface for the Zinwa Q25 under LineageOS 24 (Android 17). Each change is a commit of its
own after v3.6 (git log v3.6..):

- the package is dev.bold7.camera.benos and Android's label is "Camera", so that it installs
  beside the original BenOS Camera without replacing it;
- no network request: the update check against api.github.com, at each start and from the
  settings, is removed with the INTERNET permission;
- focus: the video use case is bound only while filming, so that the photo keeps CameraX's
  continuous picture autofocus; before each photo the lens focuses on the centre, or on the point
  last touched (touch to focus, new); the trackpad's click and Enter focus, then shoot;
- a sharper viewfinder: with the 4:3 setting the preview stream is a 720x720 square, the size
  of the Q25's screen, shown pixel for pixel, instead of 640x480 enlarged 1.5 times;
- the first press of Enter or of the trackpad after touching the screen shoots: no view of the
  camera takes the focus any more, which swallowed that press;
- full screen: the camera hides the system bars (a swipe from the edge shows them for a moment),
  so that nothing lies under Android 17's status bar, and the settings and about screens stay
  below it;
- no proprietary library: the QR and barcode reader is ZXing's (core 3.5.3, Apache License 2.0,
  https://github.com/zxing/zxing) instead of Google's ML Kit; ML Kit's automatic zoom on a
  far code goes with it, and its setting;
- 3.6-bold7.2: one photo per press of a shutter key (the trackpad's click, Enter, Space, the
  camera and volume keys), all read in one place: a held key no longer shoots again every
  second, and a second press of a key, or of another shutter key, before the first is released
  does not shoot; no toast after a photo or a video, as on the BlackBerry 9900, which only shows
  the picture taken.

Third-party libraries in the APK, all under the Apache License 2.0: AndroidX (CameraX, AppCompat,
Preference, Core, Lifecycle, Media3), Material Components for Android, Kotlin and its coroutines,
ZXing.
